WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.701
I think Donald Trump wants Nikki

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:03.703
Haley in the 2024 race.

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:04.437
This week, Haley,

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:04.938
a former South

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:05.739
Carolina governor

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:07.007
and Trump's former ambassador

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.041
to the U.N.,

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:09.509
announced her intention to run

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.977
with a formal announcement to come later

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:11.878
this month.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:12.612
This despite

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.514
previously pledging that she wouldn't do

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.649
so if Trump were running again.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.284
President Trump has been withering

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:18.918
about people

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:20.453
who he endorsed or appointed

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.155
becoming his opponents.

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.424
Witness Ronda sanctimonious.

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.126
And yet, when it comes to Haley,

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:27.961
he seems less concerned.

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:28.495
Here's what he said

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:29.662
on his campaign plane.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.464
Last weekend.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:34.734
Nikki Haley called me the other day

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:35.635
to talk to me.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.203
I said, look, you know, go buy your heart

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:38.071
if you want to run.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.205
She's publicly saying,

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.808
I would never run against my president.

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:42.909
He was a great president.

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:45.812
Go buy your heart.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.881
He said, I, for one, am not surprised.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.382
I don't think he sees her as a threat.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.917
In fact, her presence

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.453
probably helps him in his mind.

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:54.788
Haley has to contend with the fact

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.089
that she may be up against her

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:57.690
state's senator, Tim Scott.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.625
Should he get in?

00:00:58.625 --> 00:00:59.793
And that Trump's

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:01.694
candidacy already has the support

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:02.562
of two top South

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.597
Carolina Republicans, its other Senator,

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:05.932
Lindsey Graham, and Governor

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.400
Henry McMaster.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.536
Also, Trump has this bloc of voters

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.137
who are seemingly locked in with him.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.439
No matter what.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:15.008
According to a recent Monmouth poll, 22%

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.576
of Republican primary voters

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:17.610
say they'll support Trump

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.946
even if he runs as an independent.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:21.915
That might be a low number.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.017
It's an immovable number, it seems.

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.019
It suggests that it could be even higher.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:26.619
Plus,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.855
if 2024 is a rematch with Joe Biden.

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:30.123
And Emerson poll recently

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.625
found that 44% said they'd support Trump.

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:35.095
41% said Biden.

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:35.929
But let's remember that

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.164
if Haley barely registers in the polls

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:40.733
thus far, it could all change on a dime.

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:42.135
Making predictions

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:43.503
this early in a presidential

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.105
race is often folly

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.139
based on polling

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.842
or even on the list of who has announced.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:49.876
Jeff Greenfield

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:50.977
pointed all of this out

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.512
in a recent Politico column

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:54.481
titled A Warning to the Media

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:56.883
About the Next Presidential Race.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:58.751
There are plenty of historical examples

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.386
of frontrunners at this stage.

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.222
Of the game who never got close.

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:03.389
Among them, you'll remember

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:04.858
in 2003,

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:06.092
Vermont Governor Howard Dean

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.727
was dominating the polls

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.129
and had won the endorsement of both

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:10.530
Al Gore and Bill Bradley,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.732
the prior contenders for the nomination.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.234
People were asking

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:15.668
if the race was already over.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.270
And then came the Iowa caucuses,

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.104
where Dean ran

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:19.506
into ran a weak

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.340
third to John

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.875
Kerry and John Edwards,

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:23.176
who ended up being the president

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.844
and VP nominees.

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:25.879
Then four years later,

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:26.679
the media was saying

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.815
that the race was America's mayor,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:29.916
Rudy Giuliani.

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.818
Against Hillary Clinton.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.153
That is, until primary voters

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.988
weighed in and nominated John McCain

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.256
and Barack Obama.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:39.359
Even as recently as this time in 2019.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.594
Joe Biden's candidacy was perceived

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.563
by many as being dead on arrival.

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:45.298
So all of this early polling

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:46.733
is to be taken with a grain of salt.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:47.767
I get it.

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.403
But for Trump to prevail as the nominee

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.905
he needs to diminish the strength

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:55.108
of any single opponent like Ron DeSantis.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.543
And to accomplish that,

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.044
he needs Nikki Haley in the race

00:02:58.044 --> 00:02:58.845
just like he needs Mike

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.047
Pence and Sununu and Hogan and

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:02.215
Christie and everybody else,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.717
as many people as he can fit

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.686
on that stage to divvy up the pie,

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.788
just like he did in 2016

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:10.590
for Donald Trump in 20, 24.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.692
It's the more the merrier.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:13.726
Joining me now is David

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:14.727
Byler,

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.596
data analyst and political columnist

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:17.664
for The Washington Post.

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:18.731
His latest piece

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:20.533
is this Trump's Republican

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.403
opponents are making a painfully obvious

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:24.904
mistake.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.673
David, Donald Trump is under fire

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:28.541
from a variety of investigations

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:29.676
and much has been written

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:30.510
about Republicans

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.545
who want to move on from him.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.513
But in this piece,

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:34.948
you point out that you think he's

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:36.983
actually in a stronger position

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:39.319
than he was at this stage in 2016.

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:40.553
How come?

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:42.422
Well, part of it

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.224
is exactly what you pointed out.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:45.391
It's that there is a chunk

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.093
of the Republican establishment

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:48.962
that is already endorsing him,

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:50.029
that already likes him,

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.631
that's already behind him.

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.433
And if you look at his polling numbers

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:54.234
at this point

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:55.134
in 2016

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.971
and the 2016 cycle compared to now,

00:03:58.171 --> 00:03:59.839
he has a higher favorability

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.941
rating than he did back then.

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:04.077
Over 70% of the Republican Party

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.445
use him favorably.

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:07.213
A lot of Republican voters

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:09.182
really liked how he governed as president

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:10.683
and really have warm

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.352
feelings towards him.

00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:13.987
So I think that you know

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:14.821
he could lose

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.189
he could go down in flames

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:17.223
as many are predicting.

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.993
But he also has a path to a majority.

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:22.161
He doesn't have the same resistance of,

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:24.631
oh, he's just a reality TV star or oh,

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.199
this is just a stunt.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.033
These last few years

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.235
have transformed him into really

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.004
the party's elder statesman, for

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:31.638
better or worse.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:35.041
So let's put up on the screen

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:36.309
where things stood

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:37.710
in the 2016 cycle

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.544
so it was literally

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.413
it was February of 2015.

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.881
He wasn't even registering.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:42.582
People like me

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:43.716
were laughing

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:44.851
at the prospect of him

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:45.718
getting in the race,

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:47.954
much less succeeding. Undecided.

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.622
Was it 21%?

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:50.957
The Bush reference

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:52.859
there, of course, is Jeb.

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:55.561
Fast forward now to where we are today

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:57.130
for comparison.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:57.830
And all of a sudden

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.500
you'll see that Donald Trump's at 46%,

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.769
Ron DeSantis at 31.

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:04.337
And I think to my point you know

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:06.939
then you've got undecided at $0.08

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:07.440
at six.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.108
Haley at three, Cruz

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:10.710
at two, Rubio at two.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:11.144
Pompeo

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.879
all those others

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:15.214
beyond Trump and beyond DeSantis.

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:18.384
They need to hope that either Trump fades

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:19.952
because he's got issues,

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.321
indictments, something else,

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:24.090
or that DeSantis has a glass jaw, right?

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:26.392
Because if Trump remains where he is

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.194
and DeSantis remains where he is,

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.228
there's not enough oxygen

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:29.929
left in the room.

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:31.698
Right.

00:05:31.698 --> 00:05:32.365
Exactly.

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:33.166
As you pointed out,

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.168
it's still very early going.

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:36.235
But at this point,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.371
the dynamics of the race are very,

00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:39.672
very different.

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.074
In that 2016 race,

00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:42.742
you had a fractured field now

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:44.744
and you kept on having a fractured field.

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:46.446
You kept on having sort of Trump

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:48.381
skeptical Republicans flipping around.

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:49.515
Do I like Ben Carson?

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:50.516
Do I like John Kasich?

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:51.651
Do I like Marco Rubio?

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:52.852
Do I like Ted Cruz?

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.021
Right now you have a two man race.

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:57.090
You have Trump with his faction

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.191
and you have the scientist

00:05:58.191 --> 00:05:58.958
sort of leading

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:00.560
the pack of Trump alternatives

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.394
and everybody else

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:02.895
in that single digit range.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:04.630
So it's just a fundamentally

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:07.166
different race at this moment.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:07.467
Okay.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.868
And that is the mistake

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.203
that you think his would be

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.971
opponents have not recognized.

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:14.173
They don't get how the dynamics

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:14.841
have shifted.

00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:16.476
Just take 15 seconds on that.

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:18.077
Yeah.

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:19.812
I mean, there's two sides of the coin.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:20.747
Trump is no longer

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:21.814
an insurgent candidate

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.316
and his opposition is no longer

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:24.650
so divided.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.752
That makes for a completely

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:27.186
different primary.

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:28.621
David Byler,

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:30.022
thank you so much for your experience

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:32.592
and Expertize. We appreciate it.

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:33.025
Thank you.

